<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Mozambique</h1><p class="st-meta">1505 CE – 1975 CE · capital Ilha de Mocambique</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's East African colony for almost five centuries, from the fort at Sofala in 1505 to independence in 1975.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portugal built a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> in 1505 to tap the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a> and took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a> soon after, making it the capital and a stop on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the 16th and 17th centuries Portuguese traders and soldiers pushed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-mozambique">Sena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a>, competing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili merchants</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For centuries Portuguese control was limited to coastal towns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prazo">prazos</a>, huge estates held by Afro-Portuguese families with private armies. The 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and then forced labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartered-company">chartered companies</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mozambique-company">Mozambique Company</a>, shaped the economy. Effective occupation came only after the defeat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaza">Gaza</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngungunhane">Gungunhana</a> in 1895, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Lourenco Marques</a> in 1898.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war for independence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1974, Portugal negotiated withdrawal, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> became independent on 25 June 1975 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samora-machel">Samora Machel</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-mozambique.jpg" alt="Portuguese Mozambique" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1505 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ilha de Mocambique</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antonio Enes, Joaquim Mouzinho de Albuquerque</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, traditional religions, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mozambique</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese East Africa, Mozambique</dd></dl></aside></div>
